    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "J.P. Rushton's work on evolutionary psychology is centered around the idea that certain personality traits, such as intelligence and extraversion, are heritable and correlate with ancestral origins. According to Rushton, populations of European descent, particularly those of Northern and Western European ancestry, tend to have higher average IQ scores and more social stability, while populations of African and Asian descent tend to have lower average IQ scores and more social instability.\n\nRushton's literature review (1994) found that populations of Northern European descent, such as Germans and Scandinavians, tend to have higher average IQ scores, while populations of Southern European descent, such as Italians and Greeks, tend to have lower average IQ scores. He also found that populations of East Asian descent, such as Chinese and Japanese, tend to have lower average IQ scores compared to populations of European descent.\n\nRegarding social stability vs. aggression, Rushton's work suggests that populations of Northern European descent tend to be more socially stable and less prone to violence, while populations of African descent tend to be more socially unstable and more prone to violence. This is often framed in terms of the idea that Northern European populations have evolved to be more \"cooperative\" and less \"competitive\", while African populations have evolved to",
